ages/90/90dcf60e19c9031927df68a1d0be6b85c47910e6ec92e9052c292f667971d7f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2756c89-5674-5f65-a5ec-2148cd18a776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90dcf60e19c9031927df68a1d0be6b85c47910e6ec92e9052c292f667971d7f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350301} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7f0b1fe-05a8-5f7f-8fe9-bd1859064717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "425d7ca7-d3cb-5410-a46b-eb55aab1c73d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81820" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6741c165a0f0f50bf932dd04c6d4c1157b4f70a8dc4c63efc5416b3883124bc.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4e/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/45/45f0ff53440247c533dd2b97ae03c8cd3e2e762b67832ce770c0bd5749e74f6e.png)"}] [{: